When hunting on web applications exclusively, Puppeteer acts as a fully functional browser core. This allows you to uncover vulnerabilities that traditional raw HTTP clients (like cURL, Burp Suite intruder, or Go-based directory busters) miss because they cannot execute complex client-side JavaScript. [1, 2, 3, 4] 
Here are all the web vulnerabilities you can effectively hunt for, automate, and chain using Puppeteer. [5, 6] 
------------------------------
## 1. Client-Side & DOM-Based Vulnerabilities
Because Puppeteer runs a real rendering engine, it excels at capturing bugs triggered inside the Document Object Model (DOM) after JavaScript executes. [7] 

* DOM-Based Cross-Site Scripting (DOM XSS): You can inject payloads into inputs or URL parameters, and use Puppeteer's page.on('dialog') or console listeners to detect if an alert(), confirm(), or unhandled script block triggers. [8, 9, 10, 11] 
* Client-Side Race Conditions: Open dozens of headless tabs simultaneously to submit a coupon code or an API checkout form at the exact same millisecond, testing if the backend database validates multi-threaded balance transfers correctly.
* Open Redirects (JavaScript-driven): Monitor page.on('framenavigated') to see if a query parameter (like ?next=//malicious.com) successfully bypasses client-side validation logic and forces the browser window to navigate off-site.
* Client-Side ReDoS (Regular Expression Denial of Service): Pass heavy strings into search boxes or profile fields. If Puppeteer's page.goto hangs indefinitely or throws a navigation timeout, you may have frozen the browser engine via an unoptimized regex evaluation.

------------------------------
## 2. Information Disclosure & Target Scoping
Modern web apps (React, Angular, Vue) load configurations dynamically into memory. Puppeteer can extract this data cleanly. [12, 13] 

* Leaked Frontend API Keys & Secrets: Programmatically scrape the global window object or parse hidden HTML attributes (data-*) for third-party service tokens (AWS buckets, Firebase keys, Stripe development tokens).
* Hidden API Endpoints: Use page.on('request') to intercept and log every single background XMLHttpRequest (XHR) or Fetch call. This exposes internal API routes, microservices, and routing patterns that are completely invisible in the raw HTML source code.
* Leaked Source Maps: Check if production builds accidentally expose source mapping files. Puppeteer can try loading .map paths to check for exposed server-side structures and developers' raw comments. [14] 

------------------------------
## 3. Authentication & Authorization Flaws
Puppeteer can handle form inputs, multi-step flows, and state management flawlessly. [15, 16] 

* Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA / IDOR): After authenticating, write a loop that changes user IDs in the URL parameter or local storage keys, then check if Puppeteer can still successfully render confidential profile pages belonging to other users.
* Weak Account Lockout / Brute-Force: Automate login forms with a massive list of passwords. Because Puppeteer behaves like a real user (generating authentic mouse movements, keystrokes, and TLS fingerprints), it bypasses simple IP or user-agent throttling blocks. [17, 18, 19, 20, 21] 
* CSRF Token Hijacking via Origin Errors: Inspect request headers to see if anti-CSRF or custom tracking headers are missing or improperly assigned when cross-origin actions are simulated by your scripts.

------------------------------
## 4. Advanced Network & Protocol Level Flaws
By tapping into Chromium’s lower-level networking layer, you can track structural backend issues.

* Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) Validation: Set up a local listening server. Use Puppeteer to trigger file uploads, webhook creations, or profile picture URL changes on the target, monitoring if the target's backend makes unauthorized connections back to your IP.
* CORS Misconfigurations: Inject a script that attempts to fetch sensitive user endpoints while changing the Origin header. If the response headers reflect Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * or allow your arbitrary origin alongside Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true, you have found a critical bug.
* Subdomain Takeovers (Visual Verification): Feed a list of thousands of subdomains into a Puppeteer script that takes quick screenshots of each one. You can rapidly look through the resulting folder to find visual signatures of abandoned cloud infrastructure (e.g., "AWS 404", "GitHub Pages NoSuchBucket"). [22, 23, 24, 25, 26] 

------------------------------
## 5. Content Security Policy (CSP) & Security Header Weaknesses

* CSP Bypasses: Evaluate how strict the site's Content Security Policy is by trying to inject arbitrary inline scripts into parameters or checking if the browser successfully evaluates scripts hosted on untrusted domains.
* Missing Defensive Headers: Read response.headers() directly from Puppeteer to evaluate if critical security flags like X-Frame-Options, Strict-Transport-Security (HSTS), or cookie flags (Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite) are missing across user dashboards. [27, 28, 29, 30, 31] 

